114 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 22 0 R/E(Hold a sprig of elder and look closely: hundreds of tiny cream flowers, each with five petals, spread into a flat, lacy circle like a doily. Lean in and you catch a soft, honey-musky smell. By late summer those flowers become heavy, drooping clusters of berries so dark they look black, and if you press one it stains your fingers deep purple. That color is a clue to the medicine inside: the purple pigments, called anthocyanins, are the part that helps our immune system fight back. We never taste the raw berries, though; they must be cooked into a tart-sweet syrup. God tucked a hidden helper inside one small berry.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

